think Followed the family _bron idiom exactly (warrant_bron): to_hex/nibble/from_hex, tokens (max 3), render/parse, fixed-point re-render check, purpose-built bounded reader with named refusals. Record: format puddle-fleet-v1 · one host line (pk-hex · capacity) in enrollment order · one world line each.
think The design honesty: the reader rebuilds through r1's own add_host/add_world, so the fleet's laws (a host once, a world once, static bounds, real capacity + name) travel WITH the bytes — a record can never rebuild a fleet r1 would refuse. Unioned puddle.PuddleError into BronError so those refusals surface by their true name on rebuild.
think The crux is the PLACEMENT surviving the wire, not merely the bytes: the recovered fleet berths to the byte-identical placement (pf[w] == pr[w] for every world), so two nodes agree on where every world runs by exchanging the roster alone. Fixed point proven (re-render reproduces the exact bytes).
